  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe cold front has exited the forecast area this afternoon with just \na few remnant showers in Deuel and Grant county. Temperatures are \ncurrently in the upper 40s to around 50 degrees and aren't expected \nto move more than a degree or two before the sun sets. Winds will \ncontinue to diminish through the afternoon becoming light and \nvariable this evening as high pressure builds in. \n\nOn Sunday, a trail of latent moisture pushes northeast into southern \nSD with shortwaves ejecting out of an upper low across the southwest \nUS that is carrying some tropical moisture. Models differ on timing \nand northward extent to this energy as it battles the sfc high to \nthe north. However, all models fill in precipitation on Monday as a \nstacked low moves out of Colorado and into the Plains. Details on \nmid and upper low placement and timing matter with this system as it \ncould bring the first accumulating snow of the season to north \ncentral SD on Monday. Models tend to have a notoriously difficult \ntime resolving H7 lows, and the caa in some model depictions seems a \nlittle wonky. Still, consensus is for cooling or stable temps in the \n30s across north central SD by Monday afternoon with increasing \ntrends on QPF amounts. Looking at an inch at most right now of wet \nsnow and probably closer to half an inch north of Hwy 12, but timing \nwill be key. \n\nAs Sunday's system finally exits early Tuesday, another will follow \nright on its heels Tuesday night, though it appears to remain \nfarther north over North Dakota at this time. High pressure settles \nin Wednesday and Thursday with upper ridging that will help push \nhighs back above normal into the upper 50s to lower 60s through the \nlatter half of the work week.",
